<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
		One entitlement is requested: the microphone (com.apple.security.device.audio-input), so that the assistant's dictation can
		listen. Under the hardened runtime that Developer ID signing turns on, macOS refuses an app the microphone without it, and
		without a word: the button simply hears nothing. It only lets the app ask; macOS still asks the person (Info.plist says
		why), and the microphone is opened only while the person has dictation on, never otherwise. The app is not sandboxed, because what it opens has to be able to run the user's own
		coding agents and Git in the user's own repositories (as the command line does), write the login service
		to ~/Library/LaunchAgents and the program to ~/.local/bin. It needs no JIT or unsigned-memory entitlement:
		Go does not use them, and the web view is Apple's, in its own process. Add an entitlement here only with a
		reason written next to it; the hardened runtime (which Developer ID signing turns on) denies the rest.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
